1
00:00:00,104 --> 00:00:00,811
Welcome

2
00:00:01,739 --> 00:00:05,421
This revision starts from the ideas underneath
the equations

3
00:00:06,359 --> 00:00:10,571
A generative model learns how probability is
distributed over possible

4
00:00:10,584 --> 00:00:14,769
observations then uses that learned structure
to produce samples

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:20,462
An observation might be an image a sentence or
one row of measurements

6
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:24,416
The symbol x usually means one observation

7
00:00:25,353 --> 00:00:27,758
Theta means the collection of learned weights

8
00:00:28,696 --> 00:00:33,967
A discriminative classifier asks given this
observation what label is likely

9
00:00:34,905 --> 00:00:40,014
A generative model asks what observations are
plausible and how can I draw one

10
00:00:40,951 --> 00:00:46,291
A generative classifier can also classify by
modeling each class and applying

11
00:00:46,304 --> 00:00:47,065
Bayes' rule

12
00:00:48,016 --> 00:00:49,823
We will connect five ideas

13
00:00:50,761 --> 00:00:53,261
An autoencoder learns reconstruction

14
00:00:54,212 --> 00:00:58,601
A variational autoencoder organizes its latent
space for sampling

15
00:00:59,552 --> 00:01:02,364
A G A N learns through a competing judge

16
00:01:03,302 --> 00:01:04,986
Diffusion learns denoising

17
00:01:05,938 --> 00:01:09,959
A Transformer learns context and next-token
probabilities

18
00:01:10,897 --> 00:01:15,720
Evaluation asks whether the resulting samples
have quality and variety

19
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:21,671
For every model keep two questions separate
what happens during training and what

20
00:01:21,685 --> 00:01:23,818
happens when we generate a new sample

21
00:01:26,072 --> 00:01:30,746
A probability distribution allocates
probability across possibilities

22
00:01:31,688 --> 00:01:34,935
For a discrete set the probabilities add to one

23
00:01:35,873 --> 00:01:37,870
A sample is one random draw

24
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:43,495
Ten samples need not reproduce the exact
probabilities but their long-run

25
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:45,017
frequencies approach them

26
00:01:45,954 --> 00:01:47,775
The vertical bar means given

27
00:01:48,726 --> 00:01:53,278
Probability of a label given an image is
different from probability of an image

28
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:54,174
given a label

29
00:01:55,112 --> 00:01:58,971
A prior is our distribution before observing
the current evidence

30
00:01:59,908 --> 00:02:02,517
A posterior incorporates that evidence

31
00:02:03,468 --> 00:02:05,126
Mean describes location

32
00:02:06,077 --> 00:02:11,077
Variance describes squared spread and standard
deviation is its square root

33
00:02:12,014 --> 00:02:15,601
Standard normal noise has mean zero and
variance one

34
00:02:16,539 --> 00:02:20,520
Its individual draws are random numbers not
always zero

35
00:02:21,471 --> 00:02:24,664
An expectation is a probability-weighted
average

36
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:29,134
In training a minibatch often estimates that
average

37
00:02:30,071 --> 00:02:34,745
The natural logarithm written l n on these
notes is the logarithm we use

38
00:02:34,759 --> 00:02:35,411
throughout

39
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:38,454
Exponentiation reverses it

40
00:02:39,406 --> 00:02:45,139
For probabilities below one the logarithm is
negative so negative log probability

41
00:02:45,153 --> 00:02:46,620
is a positive penalty

42
00:02:47,571 --> 00:02:51,647
Keep these definitions nearby they reappear in
every chapter

43
00:02:53,888 --> 00:02:58,168
A neural network is a differentiable
computation with adjustable weights

44
00:02:59,110 --> 00:03:04,749
It receives inputs computes an output and
receives a loss a number measuring how

45
00:03:04,762 --> 00:03:07,616
undesirable that output is under our objective

46
00:03:08,567 --> 00:03:11,107
A derivative measures local sensitivity

47
00:03:12,045 --> 00:03:15,482
If one weight changes slightly how does the
loss change

48
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:20,061
With many weights the collection of derivatives
is the gradient

49
00:03:20,999 --> 00:03:25,414
Gradient descent updates each weight by
subtracting the learning rate times its

50
00:03:25,428 --> 00:03:26,162
derivative

51
00:03:27,113 --> 00:03:31,107
A negative derivative therefore increases the
weight during this update

52
00:03:32,045 --> 00:03:34,450
Backpropagation applies the chain rule

53
00:03:35,387 --> 00:03:40,863
When a weight influences an intermediate code
which influences the output multiply

54
00:03:40,876 --> 00:03:42,996
the sensitivities along that path

55
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:48,213
When multiple paths reach the same quantity add
their contributions

56
00:03:49,151 --> 00:03:51,447
A vector is an ordered list of numbers

57
00:03:52,385 --> 00:03:56,257
A dot product multiplies corresponding entries
and adds them

58
00:03:57,208 --> 00:04:01,243
Matrix multiplication performs collections of
dot products

59
00:04:02,181 --> 00:04:04,952
It is different from elementwise multiplication

60
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:09,001
You do not need to remember a whole calculus
course

61
00:04:09,952 --> 00:04:15,822
Remember local sensitivity multiply along paths
add across paths and subtract

62
00:04:15,836 --> 00:04:16,583
the gradient

63
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:21,121
We will use an actual autoencoder calculation
shortly

64
00:04:23,384 --> 00:04:25,436
Here is the lecture's spam example

65
00:04:26,391 --> 00:04:31,310
S means spam H means ordinary mail and W means
the word win

66
00:04:32,247 --> 00:04:34,544
Forty percent of messages are spam

67
00:04:35,495 --> 00:04:37,492
Sixty percent are ordinary

68
00:04:38,429 --> 00:04:43,090
The word appears in sixty percent of spam and
five percent of ordinary mail

69
00:04:44,027 --> 00:04:46,432
First multiply likelihood by prior

70
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:52,845
The spam-and-win joint probability is zero
point six times zero point four giving

71
00:04:52,859 --> 00:04:54,299
zero point two four

72
00:04:55,250 --> 00:05:00,780
The ordinary-and-win joint is zero point zero
five times zero point six giving

73
00:05:00,794 --> 00:05:02,342
zero point zero three

74
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:06,201
Now normalize across the two explanations

75
00:05:07,139 --> 00:05:10,726
Total probability of seeing win is zero point
two seven

76
00:05:11,663 --> 00:05:16,527
The spam posterior is zero point two four
divided by zero point two seven

77
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:19,598
approximately zero point eight eight eight nine

78
00:05:20,535 --> 00:05:25,087
The ordinary posterior is approximately zero
point one one one one

79
00:05:26,025 --> 00:05:27,112
They sum to one

80
00:05:28,063 --> 00:05:30,644
Why is zero point two four not the answer

81
00:05:31,582 --> 00:05:34,707
It is a joint probability before normalization

82
00:05:35,644 --> 00:05:40,006
Bayes turns joint scores into probabilities
conditional on the evidence

83
00:05:40,957 --> 00:05:46,636
Also probability of spam without win is not one
minus probability of spam with win

84
00:05:47,573 --> 00:05:49,693
Those are different conditioning events

85
00:05:51,944 --> 00:05:54,499
A model needs both fidelity and coverage

86
00:05:55,441 --> 00:05:58,769
Fidelity means its outputs resemble real
examples

87
00:05:59,707 --> 00:06:04,435
Coverage means it represents the different
kinds of examples that occur in reality

88
00:06:05,386 --> 00:06:11,025
In this table real data has probability zero in
region A zero point four in

89
00:06:11,038 --> 00:06:13,756
B and zero point six in C

90
00:06:14,707 --> 00:06:20,033
The model puts zero point two in A nothing in B
and zero point eight in C

91
00:06:20,359 --> 00:06:22,669
Region A is invalid generation

92
00:06:23,606 --> 00:06:27,927
Region B is a missing real possibility also
called a missing mode

93
00:06:28,878 --> 00:06:33,538
A hundred model draws have expected counts
twenty zero and eighty

94
00:06:34,489 --> 00:06:39,408
Drawing more samples cannot recover a region
assigned exactly zero probability

95
00:06:40,345 --> 00:06:45,373
Conversely a mode missing from a small sample
grid does not prove its probability

96
00:06:45,386 --> 00:06:46,079
is zero

97
00:06:47,017 --> 00:06:52,655
Older generative families include Naive Bayes
Gaussian mixtures and hidden Markov

98
00:06:52,669 --> 00:06:53,280
models

99
00:06:54,218 --> 00:06:58,144
Our neural families approach the same
distribution problem through different

100
00:06:58,158 --> 00:06:59,245
training tasks

101
00:07:00,182 --> 00:07:04,992
Remember the distinction between a believable
individual output and a good overall

102
00:07:05,006 --> 00:07:05,889
distribution

103
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:12,329
We will return to it when diagnosing G A Ns and
calculating evaluation scores

104
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:18,785
An autoencoder receives an observation and
tries to reproduce it

105
00:07:19,727 --> 00:07:25,501
Follow the diagram input x passes through an
encoder producing a latent code z

106
00:07:26,453 --> 00:07:30,515
A decoder maps that code to a reconstruction
called x hat

107
00:07:31,453 --> 00:07:34,183
Both networks learn from reconstruction error

108
00:07:35,121 --> 00:07:37,553
The code is a compact representation

109
00:07:38,504 --> 00:07:41,833
A bottleneck encourages it to preserve useful
structure

110
00:07:42,784 --> 00:07:48,341
Nonlinear networks can learn nonlinear
representations unlike ordinary linear

111
00:07:48,355 --> 00:07:50,338
principal component analysis

112
00:07:51,276 --> 00:07:54,224
The same pattern supports several applications

113
00:07:55,162 --> 00:08:00,094
A denoising autoencoder receives corrupted
input but targets the clean image

114
00:08:01,031 --> 00:08:04,414
Colorization can receive grayscale and target
color

115
00:08:05,352 --> 00:08:08,124
Retrieval compares latent representations

116
00:08:09,061 --> 00:08:14,020
Anomaly detection can flag high reconstruction
error although that heuristic can

117
00:08:14,034 --> 00:08:14,537
fail

118
00:08:15,488 --> 00:08:19,048
An important limitation appears when we ask for
generation

119
00:08:19,999 --> 00:08:24,577
Reconstruction training teaches the decoder at
the codes reached by the encoder

120
00:08:25,515 --> 00:08:29,741
It does not impose a known distribution over
every possible latent point

121
00:08:30,678 --> 00:08:34,401
Arbitrary random codes can land in poorly
trained gaps

122
00:08:35,338 --> 00:08:40,800
Before solving that problem with a V A E let us
calculate one small autoencoder

123
00:08:40,814 --> 00:08:42,037
and watch a weight learn

124
00:08:44,288 --> 00:08:49,546
This is a practice example from your
handwritten notes using two input values and

125
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:50,661
one latent number

126
00:08:51,616 --> 00:08:53,505
The input is one zero

127
00:08:54,456 --> 00:08:59,999
The encoder computes a times the first input
plus b times the second input plus

128
00:09:00,013 --> 00:09:00,420
c

129
00:09:01,372 --> 00:09:07,064
The initial a is zero point five b is zero
point two five and c is zero point

130
00:09:07,078 --> 00:09:07,486
one

131
00:09:08,423 --> 00:09:13,790
The code is therefore zero point five times one
plus zero point two five times

132
00:09:13,804 --> 00:09:17,486
zero plus zero point one zero point six

133
00:09:18,437 --> 00:09:23,640
The first reconstruction is zero point eight
times the code plus zero point one

134
00:09:23,939 --> 00:09:25,733
giving zero point five eight

135
00:09:26,684 --> 00:09:32,418
The second is negative zero point five times
the code plus zero point six giving

136
00:09:32,431 --> 00:09:33,600
zero point three

137
00:09:34,551 --> 00:09:36,521
Subtract the original input

138
00:09:37,458 --> 00:09:41,562
The errors are negative zero point four two and
zero point three

139
00:09:42,513 --> 00:09:48,165
Square and add zero point one seven six four
plus zero point zero nine equals

140
00:09:48,179 --> 00:09:50,352
zero point two six six four

141
00:09:51,304 --> 00:09:52,852
That is sum squared error

142
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:58,736
Mean squared error divides by the two
coordinates giving zero point one three

143
00:09:58,749 --> 00:09:59,551
three two

144
00:10:00,502 --> 00:10:02,418
State which reduction you use

145
00:10:04,664 --> 00:10:09,189
For this sum squared error the derivative at
each reconstructed coordinate is

146
00:10:09,202 --> 00:10:10,221
twice its error

147
00:10:11,163 --> 00:10:15,538
We get negative zero point eight four and
positive zero point six

148
00:10:16,476 --> 00:10:20,348
Both reconstructed coordinates depend on the
same latent code

149
00:10:21,286 --> 00:10:26,163
The first path contributes negative zero point
eight four times zero point eight

150
00:10:27,101 --> 00:10:32,305
The second contributes positive zero point six
times negative zero point five

151
00:10:33,242 --> 00:10:34,995
Add the two contributions

152
00:10:35,932 --> 00:10:39,723
The latent derivative is negative zero point
nine seven two

153
00:10:40,661 --> 00:10:44,601
The encoder code depends on a through the first
input which is one

154
00:10:45,538 --> 00:10:50,688
So the derivative with respect to a is also
negative zero point nine seven two

155
00:10:51,625 --> 00:10:56,449
At learning rate zero point one subtract that
derivative times the rate from the

156
00:10:56,462 --> 00:11:01,992
original a Zero point five minus zero point one
times negative zero point nine

157
00:11:02,006 --> 00:11:05,212
seven two gives zero point five nine seven two

158
00:11:06,163 --> 00:11:08,853
What made this calculation easy to get wrong

159
00:11:09,791 --> 00:11:11,639
There are two decoder paths

160
00:11:12,576 --> 00:11:17,359
We must add both and compute all derivatives
using the original forward-pass

161
00:11:17,372 --> 00:11:19,492
weights before applying the update

162
00:11:20,430 --> 00:11:24,723
Changing from summed error to mean error would
also change gradient scale

163
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:31,784
A variational autoencoder addresses latent
sampling by changing what the encoder

164
00:11:31,797 --> 00:11:33,740
outputs and how it is trained

165
00:11:34,682 --> 00:11:40,063
Instead of one deterministic code the encoder
outputs parameters of a probability

166
00:11:40,076 --> 00:11:41,856
distribution for this input

167
00:11:42,794 --> 00:11:48,215
In the common diagonal Gaussian case it outputs
a mean vector mu and a log

168
00:11:48,228 --> 00:11:49,709
variance vector ell

169
00:11:50,647 --> 00:11:55,891
The approximate latent distribution is called q
phi of z given x Phi names the

170
00:11:55,905 --> 00:11:56,965
encoder weights

171
00:11:57,916 --> 00:12:02,712
A prior commonly a standard normal distribution
is called p of z

172
00:12:03,663 --> 00:12:07,889
We sample a latent code from the encoder's
distribution and the decoder

173
00:12:07,902 --> 00:12:09,492
reconstructs the input

174
00:12:10,429 --> 00:12:12,861
The decoder weights are often named theta

175
00:12:13,799 --> 00:12:18,826
If the latent code has two coordinates the mean
has two values and log variance

176
00:12:18,840 --> 00:12:20,049
has two values

177
00:12:20,986 --> 00:12:26,109
Sampling combines those parameters into a two-
coordinate code the decoder does not

178
00:12:26,122 --> 00:12:27,617
receive four coordinates

179
00:12:28,568 --> 00:12:33,976
For reconstruction we start from a specific
input and use its encoder distribution

180
00:12:34,927 --> 00:12:38,826
For new generation sample z from the prior and
decode it

181
00:12:39,777 --> 00:12:43,486
The generation path needs no input image and no
encoder

182
00:12:45,728 --> 00:12:48,038
Use the handwritten values on screen

183
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:51,100
The mean is one negative one

184
00:12:52,051 --> 00:12:57,309
Log variances are the natural logarithm of zero
point two five and the natural

185
00:12:57,322 --> 00:12:58,518
logarithm of four

186
00:12:59,469 --> 00:13:04,061
The sampled standard normal noise is two
negative zero point five

187
00:13:04,999 --> 00:13:09,917
Exponentiating log variance gives variances
zero point two five and four

188
00:13:10,855 --> 00:13:15,189
Taking square roots gives standard deviations
zero point five and two

189
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:19,659
Equivalently exponentiate half the log variance

190
00:13:20,610 --> 00:13:26,222
The reparameterized sample is mean plus
standard deviation times noise coordinate

191
00:13:26,235 --> 00:13:27,241
by coordinate

192
00:13:28,178 --> 00:13:32,798
First coordinate one plus zero point five times
two equals two

193
00:13:33,749 --> 00:13:39,374
Second negative one plus two times negative
zero point five equals negative two

194
00:13:40,312 --> 00:13:43,436
The sampled code is therefore two negative two

195
00:13:44,374 --> 00:13:49,455
Variance standard deviation and log variance
are three different quantities

196
00:13:50,393 --> 00:13:54,034
The decoder receives the sampled code not the
noise alone

197
00:13:54,972 --> 00:13:56,874
Why did we draw noise separately

198
00:13:57,825 --> 00:14:02,472
It lets us describe the random code as a
differentiable transformation of learned

199
00:14:02,485 --> 00:14:03,409
mean and scale

200
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:09,523
During backpropagation hold the particular
noise draw fixed and differentiate that

201
00:14:09,537 --> 00:14:10,556
transformation

202
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:17,787
The V A E objective needs a way to encourage
encoder distributions to resemble the

203
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:18,317
prior

204
00:14:19,258 --> 00:14:23,239
It uses K L divergence named after Kullback and
Leibler

205
00:14:24,177 --> 00:14:27,329
It is a directional measure of distribution
mismatch

206
00:14:28,267 --> 00:14:33,769
For discrete distributions P and Q take each
probability from P multiply

207
00:14:33,783 --> 00:14:38,701
it by the natural logarithm of P divided by Q
then sum across outcomes

208
00:14:39,653 --> 00:14:42,207
The first distribution supplies the weights

209
00:14:43,144 --> 00:14:46,908
Reversing direction changes both the ratios and
the weights

210
00:14:47,859 --> 00:14:50,685
K L is therefore not generally symmetric

211
00:14:51,623 --> 00:14:56,840
Individual weighted terms can be negative
because a probability ratio can be below

212
00:14:56,854 --> 00:14:57,288
one

213
00:14:58,226 --> 00:15:00,807
The complete divergence cannot be negative

214
00:15:01,758 --> 00:15:05,087
If the distributions match the divergence is
zero

215
00:15:06,025 --> 00:15:11,704
On this page P has probabilities zero point
three six zero point four eight

216
00:15:11,949 --> 00:15:13,674
and zero point one six

217
00:15:14,612 --> 00:15:17,356
Q is uniform over three possibilities

218
00:15:18,294 --> 00:15:22,900
The forward divergence is approximately zero
point zero eight five three

219
00:15:23,851 --> 00:15:29,408
Reversing direction gives approximately zero
point zero nine seven four five five

220
00:15:30,345 --> 00:15:35,264
For the V A E we use the encoder distribution
as the first distribution and the

221
00:15:35,278 --> 00:15:36,609
prior as the second

222
00:15:38,864 --> 00:15:43,661
For one coordinate let q be a Gaussian with
mean mu and variance v

223
00:15:44,616 --> 00:15:46,396
Let p be standard normal

224
00:15:47,347 --> 00:15:52,904
By definition K L is the expectation under q of
log q minus log p

225
00:15:53,855 --> 00:15:56,518
Subtract the two Gaussian log densities

226
00:15:57,456 --> 00:15:59,453
Their common constant cancels

227
00:16:00,390 --> 00:16:05,526
What remains is negative one half log v minus
the squared deviation from mu

228
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:09,385
divided by two v plus z squared divided by two

229
00:16:10,336 --> 00:16:11,899
Now average under q

230
00:16:12,850 --> 00:16:16,355
The expected squared deviation from its own
mean is v

231
00:16:17,306 --> 00:16:20,350
Therefore the middle term becomes negative one
half

232
00:16:21,287 --> 00:16:26,722
The expected value of z squared is variance
plus mean squared giving v plus mu

233
00:16:26,736 --> 00:16:27,320
squared

234
00:16:28,271 --> 00:16:29,996
Combine these three pieces

235
00:16:30,948 --> 00:16:35,608
We obtain one half times v plus mu squared
minus one minus log v

236
00:16:36,559 --> 00:16:40,567
For a diagonal Gaussian add this expression
over coordinates

237
00:16:41,518 --> 00:16:47,034
If the network gives log variance ell replace v
with exp ell and log v with ell

238
00:16:47,986 --> 00:16:52,795
A quick correctness check put mean zero and
variance one into the expression

239
00:16:53,733 --> 00:16:56,097
Everything cancels giving zero

240
00:16:57,048 --> 00:16:59,874
A negative final K L signals a mistake

241
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:05,273
Return to the same distribution used for our
sample

242
00:17:06,214 --> 00:17:11,676
Its mean is one negative one and variances are
zero point two five and four

243
00:17:12,627 --> 00:17:18,348
For coordinate one substitute into one half
times variance plus mean squared minus

244
00:17:18,361 --> 00:17:20,100
one minus log variance

245
00:17:21,038 --> 00:17:26,309
That is one half times zero point two five plus
one minus one minus the logarithm

246
00:17:26,323 --> 00:17:27,913
of zero point two five

247
00:17:28,864 --> 00:17:32,926
It gives approximately zero point eight one
eight one four seven

248
00:17:33,877 --> 00:17:37,777
For coordinate two use variance four and mean
squared one

249
00:17:38,728 --> 00:17:42,736
We get approximately one point three zero six
eight five three

250
00:17:43,687 --> 00:17:47,206
